so far we've covered three strategies  for scaling your serverless apps  lucky for critter junction we have three  more upper sleeves  stay tuned  to review critter junction's new wallet  app lets users send and receive virtual  currency  they're using cloud functions to provide  response to queries  around wallet amounts but they hit their  database backend rate limits so they  couldn't experience the skill that  serverless provides  and wondered what other tips they were  missing so my team came in and helped  them set up  instant scaling limits on cloud  functions cloud tasks to limit the rate  of work done  and stateful storage to defer results  from long-running operations  let's explore three more tips number  four  use redis to rate limit requests  sometimes you need to perform rate  limiting in the context of the http  request  for example you might want to protect  your system from abusive users with  per user rate limits like 100 wallet  queries per minute  to control the flow of requests to your  serverless workloads  or you might need a back pressure to the  collar of your serverless workload  like if your wallet transactions called  an api  that checked for fraud which has a limit  of 100 requests per second  you need a defense measure to ensure all  your surplus functions respect that  limit and prevent the api from excessive  use  and redis is great for doing this it  gives you a very low latency  key value data store plus basic data  structures and operations that make it  one of the preferred backing source for  rate limiting  google cloud offers a fully managed  redis service called cloud memory store  if you want to learn how to use redis to  limit cloud function  invocations check out the link below  tip 5 use cloud pubsub to process the  work in batches  when you're dealing with a large number  of messages you might not want to  process  every message a common pattern is to  wait until messages have accumulated  cloud functions integrate with cloud pub  sub a messaging and ingestion middleware  instead of invoking a cloud function for  every message  serverless workloads can use pub sub as  a place to accumulate batches of work  since the service will store messages  for up to seven days  then you can use cloud scheduler a  managed pron job service to process  these items on schedule  you can even trigger the batch processes  based on the number and age of  accumulated messages  this will help you control the flow and  your functions can better handle  increases in trigger requests and  finally our last tip  use cloud run for heavily i o bound work  one of the most expensive components of  infrastructure is compute cycle  when your serverless workload is just  waiting for a remote api call or waiting  for files to read  these are billable moments cloudrun a  managed platform for running stateless  containers  lets you specify how many concurrent  requests it can handle  this can mean big increases in  efficiency for i o bound workloads  for example if the work being done  spends most of its time waiting for  replies from slow remote  api calls cloud run supports up to 80  concurrent requests  on the same instance which shares the  same cpu allocation  what you want to do is one tune your  service performance for the maximum  stable concurrency  two set your expected level of  concurrency  in any code level configuration and  deploy your service  three set cloud run concurrency equal to  or less than  any code level configuration four then  use load testing tools that support a  configurable concurrency  and make sure that your service remains  stable  critter junction now has six strategies  for their serverless scaling  in their back pocket they can use rate  limiting  set instant scaling limits and products  like cloud run  cloud task cloud scheduler and cloud pub  sub to help manage  load on their serverless instance even  highly scalable systems should have  limits on consumption  to reduce the chance of cascading  failures and maximize  throw put for more details on these tips  and an awesome decision tree  check out the link below that's a wrap  for season of scale  stay tuned for more strategies for  scaling on google cloud  and remember always be architecting  [Music]  you
 

